For Immediate Release
August 11, 2003
For more information, or to request a review copy or schedule an interview 
with the authors, contact Leigh Sacks at leigh@nostarch.com or 415-863-9900

CREATE AMAZING LEGO MODELS ON YOUR COMPUTER
No Starch Press releases the official LDraw.org guide, *Virtual LEGO*

San Francisco, CA - While there is certainly something to be said for the 
satisfying snap of interlocking plastic pieces, not all LEGO� models have 
to be made of bricks. With the LDraw suite of software you can build 
virtual LEGO models on a computer, instead of using bricks.

"Why not just play with the real thing?" you might ask. Simple: LDraw makes 
possible LEGO projects that would take an unreasonable amount of time, 
money, or storage bin space to build with physical LEGO pieces. Also, with 
LDraw LEGO enthusiasts can create custom building instructions like the 
ones that come in LEGO sets. That way a LEGO fan can document that 
fantastic castle and build it again a year from now, or she can show her 
friend in Norway how he can make the same castle for himself. Pretty cool.

Hitting bookstores in September is the official guide to LDraw, *Virtual 
LEGO* (Courtney/Bliss/Herrera, No Starch Press, US $39.95 w/CD). Written by 
the experts at LDraw.org - the hub of virtual LEGO activity - *Virtual 
LEGO* is a complete guide to the LDraw system of tools for Windows, 
including the LDraw parts library, MLCad, POV-Ray, L3P, L3PAO, LPub, 
LSynth, and more. Readers learn how to document LEGO models in MLCad, a 
powerful CAD program specifically designed to work with the free LDraw 
library of LEGO parts, as well as how to render models with POV-Ray and 
MegaPOV, free tools that can create stunning 3-D graphics. The companion 
CD-ROM provides all the software referenced in the book, as well as working 
files from chapters and a gallery of LDraw creations by LEGO builders.

Co-author Tim Courtney, who helped found LDraw.org in 1999, explains why 
this book came to be: "I chose to write it because it was an incredible 
opportunity for the LDraw community. This book is a robust tutorial that 
will answer the questions of many fans, and empower them to create their 
own 3D models." LEGO fans are already expressing their approval: "This book 
is great for new and advanced users alike, making it possible for anyone to 
get set up, build great virtual models, and output building instructions. 
Great work!" says LEGO trains enthusiast and the webmaster of Building 
Instructions Portal, Jake McKee.

With adults all over the country revisiting their childhood pastimes - note 
the surge in popularity of comic books (a.k.a. graphic novels) and the 
reappearance of television shows and toys from decades past - the 
popularity of LEGO will only grow. Membership in LEGO user groups and LEGO 
trains clubs has been swelling for the last few years and adult fans of 
LEGO are pursuing new ways to enjoy this classic hobby. *Virtual LEGO* is 
the perfect tool for that dedicated LEGO lover, no matter what their age.

VIRTUAL LEGO
The Official LDraw.org Guide to LDraw Tools for Windows
by Tim Courtney, Steve Bliss, Ahui Herrera
ISBN 1886411948, September 2003, US $39.95 ($59.95 Cdn), 456 pp. w/CD
Available at fine bookstores everywhere
To order from the publisher: visit www.nostarch.com, email 
orders@nostarch.com, or call 800-420-7240

